# one-panel, production. Copy to `.env.production`, fill in, and keep it out of git.
#
#   cp .env.production.example .env.production
#   docker compose --env-file .env.production up -d --build
#
# Every variable below is required unless marked optional. The panel validates them at boot
# and refuses to start on a bad one, with the variable named — that is deliberate, and it is why
# there are no silent defaults for anything that matters.

# ── The database ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
POSTGRES_USER=onepanel
POSTGRES_DB=onepanel
# Generate: openssl rand -base64 24
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# The host is the compose SERVICE name, not localhost — containers resolve each other by name.
#
# The password here is the SAME ONE as POSTGRES_PASSWORD above, written out a second time:
# compose creates the database with that variable and the panel connects with this URL. Filling
# in one and not the other is the easiest mistake on this page to make and the hardest to see,
# because the placeholder is in the middle of a string rather than on a line of its own —
# `scripts/preflight.sh` compares them for that reason. A password containing reserved
# characters must be percent-encoded here and not there.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://onepanel:CHANGE_ME_SAME_AS_POSTGRES_PASSWORD@postgres:5432/onepanel

# ── The irreplaceable one ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# 32 bytes, base64. Seals region secrets, TOTP enrolments and IdP client secrets.
#
# Generate: openssl rand -base64 32
#
# LOSING THIS is not an outage, it is data loss: every region secret must be re-entered by an
# operator who still knows it, and every MFA enrolment must be redone. Back it up somewhere that
# is not the same disk as the database. Rotating it is a procedure, not an edit — ask us first.
PANEL_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# ── Addresses ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The domain Caddy gets a certificate for. Must already resolve to this host.
PANEL_DOMAIN=panel.example.com

# The CSRF allow-list: which origins may submit a write. Comma-separated, each a BARE origin
# (scheme://host, no path, no trailing slash). In production this is normally exactly one, and it
# must be the https one — over http the session cookie is set and never sent back.
PANEL_ORIGIN=https://panel.example.com

# The single canonical address, used to BUILD links: invitation emails, the OIDC redirect URI you
# register with Okta, post-callback redirects. Must be one of the PANEL_ORIGIN entries.
# Optional: defaults to the first PANEL_ORIGIN entry, which is right when there is only one.
#
# It is ALSO what a load balancer appliance is told to call to say it is alive — a VM on a tenant
# network, not on yours. If an appliance cannot reach this address it boots, installs, calls it
# for ever, and the load balancer stays in "building" with nothing to say why.
#
# Settable from Admin → Settings → Panel address instead; setting it here makes that read-only.
PANEL_PUBLIC_URL=https://panel.example.com

# ── TLS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Which of the four configs in ./caddy runs. They differ in where the certificate comes from and —
# the part that is easy to get silently wrong — where the CLIENT'S ADDRESS comes from. Read the
# file you pick before deploying anything but the default.
#
#   acme        (default) Caddy gets and renews a Let's Encrypt certificate. Needs PANEL_DOMAIN
#               already resolving here and ports 80 + 443 reachable from the internet.
#   own-cert    You supply TLS_CERT_DIR/panel.crt (leaf + chain) and panel.key. Caddy does not
#               renew them; replacing an expired pair is on you.
#   cloudflare  Behind Cloudflare's proxy. Uses a Cloudflare Origin Certificate from the same two
#               filenames, refuses connections from outside Cloudflare's ranges, and takes the
#               client address from CF-Connecting-IP — without which every user of the panel would
#               share one login rate-limit bucket.
#   tunnel      Cloudflare Tunnel. No certificate and NO INBOUND PORTS AT ALL — cloudflared dials
#               out. Requires TUNNEL_TOKEN below and the extra compose file:
#                 docker compose --env-file .env.production \
#                   -f compose.yaml -f compose.tunnel.yaml up -d
TLS_MODE=acme

# Where panel.crt and panel.key live, for the two modes that need them. Ignored under `acme` and
# `tunnel`. A path outside the working copy is better — `.gitignore` covers `certs/` but the safest
# private key is one that was never in a directory git looks at.
TLS_CERT_DIR=./certs

# TLS_MODE=tunnel only. Zero Trust -> Networks -> Tunnels -> Create a tunnel; copy the connector
# token. Then add a Public Hostname on that tunnel for PANEL_DOMAIN pointing at `http://caddy:80`
# — NOT `http://panel:3000`, which would hand the login rate limiter an address the caller chose.
#
# This is a CREDENTIAL: anything holding it can serve traffic as this tunnel. Treat it like the
# database password, not like a hostname.
TUNNEL_TOKEN=

# ── Optional ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Session cookie lifetime in hours. Default 12.
SESSION_TTL_HOURS=12

# ── Where the images come from ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   build   compile them on this host. Needs roughly 4 GB of free memory for ten minutes.
#   pull    fetch published images from OMNICP_REGISTRY. Needs a `docker login` to it.
#
# `build` is the default because it is what a checkout can always do. Prefer `pull` on any host
# that also runs something else. A cold build wants roughly 4 GB and ten minutes, and a machine
# that cannot spare them can be pushed into rebooting itself mid-deploy. The panel is usually
# installed on exactly that kind of machine.
# OMNICP_SOURCE=build

# The registry holding `omnicp-panel` and `omnicp-tools`. Two images serve all six services.
# Private, and therefore the thing a licence actually controls — losing access stops UPDATES,
# never a running deployment.
#
#   echo $GITHUB_PAT | docker login ghcr.io -u <github-username> --password-stdin
#
# OMNICP_REGISTRY=ghcr.io/armar-reggans

# ── A tag when pulling, written for you when building ────────────────────────────────
#
# Under `OMNICP_SOURCE=pull` this is REQUIRED and is the image tag — an exact release such as
# `v2026.10.28a`. No moving tag is ever published, so there is no `latest` to drift onto: an
# upgrade is editing this line, which means somebody chose it.
#
# Under `build` it is set for you — `scripts/update.sh` exports it from
# `git describe --tags --always` and leaving it blank here is correct.
#
# Either way compose passes it to the migrate step, which writes it to `DeploymentEvent`, so
# the database records which build it received. A hand-run `docker compose up` with it unset
# records `unknown`, which is accurate and is why there is no default.
# OMNICP_VERSION=

# ── Uptime monitoring ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# All five have working defaults. `docker compose up -d` brings monitoring up with none of
# them set, because the worker registers itself against the database it already holds a
# credential for. They are listed so that a deployment which needs to differ knows what it is
# allowed to change.

# The worker's identity across restarts, and the location it claims checks for. A SECOND
# worker needs its own name, or the two rotate each other's token on every boot and fight over
# one row. Defaults: `local` in location `default`.
# MONITOR_WORKER_NAME=local
# MONITOR_LOCATION=default

# Set ONLY for a worker that cannot reach this database — one in another region, which is the
# case the token design exists for. Register it first and paste what it prints:
#
#   docker compose --env-file .env.production run --rm tools \
#     pnpm monitor:register -- --name worker-2 --location eu-west
#
# A CREDENTIAL. Left unset, the local worker mints and rotates its own on every start and
# writes it nowhere, which is strictly safer than a value in this file.
# MONITOR_WORKER_TOKEN=

# Where the worker finds the panel and the engine, over the compose network. Neither leaves the
# host, so neither needs a certificate or a public name. Change these only when the worker runs
# somewhere the compose network does not reach.
# MONITOR_PANEL_URL=http://panel:3000
# MONITOR_GATUS_URL=http://gatus:8080

# The nameserver monitoring uses is NOT here: it is a panel setting at /admin/monitoring,
# because it has to reach the authorisation path and the engine together. See
# `compose.override.yaml.example`.

# ── Deliberately absent ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Region endpoints and their secrets. A region is a ROW, not configuration: it is added
# through /admin/regions, and its `serveradmin` secret — which is `oneadmin`-equivalent for that
# zone — is sealed in the database under PANEL_ENCRYPTION_KEY. Putting it here instead would place
# a tier-0 credential in a file that gets copied between machines.
#
# The exception is a region whose secret is supplied by reference: `Region.secretRef` names an
# environment variable, falling back to ONE_SERVER_SECRET. Prefer the sealed route.
